  {
   "id": "8W4GLbMzAAAJ",
   "from": "joh4nd",
   "author": "7cd3b23b889525f0",
   "date": "2023-06-12T12:28:53Z",
   "subject": "Re: Robust/scaled statistics in CFA on ordinal response data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div><div>@Terrence Jorgensen, do you mind (five years later) expanding a bit\r\n on the difference between scaled and robust versions of the fit \r\ncriteria?</div><div><br></div><div>As far as I understand, the criteria \r\nlabelled &quot;scaled&quot; are old, while the &quot;robust&quot; are new and based in the \r\nevidence provided by the two papers by Brosseau-Liard and various \r\ncolleagues.</div><div><br></div><div>Would you agree that the scaled \r\nversions are for categorial variables but not necessarily robust \r\nestimates, while the robust versions are for robust estimates in \r\ngeneral? So that would mean applying any of the below estimators to fit a\r\n model would immediately recommend interpreting the robust fitmeasures()\r\n described in lavOptions (underlined in the quotation below)?<br></div><br></div><div><span style=\"background-color:gray\"><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.3333px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial\"><p style=\"color:white\">From <span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.3333px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(35,37,37);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;display:inline;float:none\">lavOptions {lavaan}:</span></p><p style=\"color:white\">&gt; The estimator to be used. Can be one of the following:<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ML&quot;</span><span> </span>for maximum likelihood,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;GLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for (normal theory) generalized least squares,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for weighted least squares (sometimes called ADF estimation),<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULS&quot;</span><span> </span>for unweighted least squares,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;DWLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for diagonally weighted least squares, and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;DLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for distributionally-weighted least squares. These are the main options that affect the estimation. For convenience, the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ML&quot;</span><span> </span>option can be extended as<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLM&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMV&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMVS&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLF&quot;</span>, and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span>. The estimation will still be plain<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ML&quot;</span>, but now with robust standard errors and a robust (scaled) test statistic. <u>For<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLM&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMV&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMVS&quot;</span>, classic robust standard errors are used (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">se=&quot;robust.sem&quot;</span>)</u>; for<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLF&quot;</span>, standard errors are based on first-order derivatives (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">information = &quot;first.order&quot;</span>);<u> for<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span>, ‘Huber-White’ robust standard errors are used (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">se=&quot;robust.huber.white&quot;</span>). </u>In addition,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLM&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a Satorra-Bentler scaled (mean adjusted) test statistic (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;satorra.bentler&quot;</span>) ,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMVS&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a mean and variance adjusted test statistic (Satterthwaite style) (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;mean.var.adjusted&quot;</span>),<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMV&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a mean and variance adjusted test statistic (scaled and shifted) (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;scaled.shifted&quot;</span>), and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a test statistic which is asymptotically equivalent to the Yuan-Bentler T2-star test statistic (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;yuan.bentler.mplus&quot;</span>). Analogously, <u>the estimators<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLSM&quot;</span><span> </span>and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLSMV&quot;</span><span> </span>imply the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;DWLS&quot;</span><span> </span>estimator (not the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLS&quot;</span><span> </span>estimator) with robust standard errors</u> and a mean or mean and variance adjusted test statistic. Estimators<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULSM&quot;</span><span> </span>and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULSMV&quot;</span><span> </span>imply the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULS&quot;</span><span> </span>estimator with robust standard errors and a mean or mean and variance adjusted test statistic.</p></span></span></div><div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Johan</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">torsdag den 13. december 2018 kl. 14.11.55 UTC+1 skrev Terrence Jorgensen:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">do you recommend reporting the non-scaled fit statistics/indices over the scaled versions? </div></blockquote><div><br></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>If robust estimation is necessary, then report the scaled statistic and any scaled (or robust, if available) fit indices that are derived from it.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div></div></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div></div></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\"><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792415191697000&amp;usg=AOvVaw100CSkGlOCJw2KAJEW0Vq9\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tzsoQ5cdAgAJ",
   "from": "cla...@googlemail.com",
   "author": "c7a6d95ac47a7abf",
   "date": "2023-12-14T10:41:01Z",
   "subject": "Re: Robust/scaled statistics in CFA on ordinal response data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>I would also be interested in the question, if reporting scaled fit indices when categorical data is used while robust test statistics are for robust estimates in general? <br></div><div><br></div><div>Looking forward to some insights on that issue!</div><div>Regards, </div><div> Claudia </div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">joh4nd schrieb am Montag, 12. Juni 2023 um 14:28:53 UTC+2:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><div>@Terrence Jorgensen, do you mind (five years later) expanding a bit\r\n on the difference between scaled and robust versions of the fit \r\ncriteria?</div><div><br></div><div>As far as I understand, the criteria \r\nlabelled &quot;scaled&quot; are old, while the &quot;robust&quot; are new and based in the \r\nevidence provided by the two papers by Brosseau-Liard and various \r\ncolleagues.</div><div><br></div><div>Would you agree that the scaled \r\nversions are for categorial variables but not necessarily robust \r\nestimates, while the robust versions are for robust estimates in \r\ngeneral? So that would mean applying any of the below estimators to fit a\r\n model would immediately recommend interpreting the robust fitmeasures()\r\n described in lavOptions (underlined in the quotation below)?<br></div><br></div><div><span style=\"background-color:gray\"><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.3333px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial\"><p style=\"color:white\">From <span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.3333px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(35,37,37);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;display:inline;float:none\">lavOptions {lavaan}:</span></p><p style=\"color:white\">&gt; The estimator to be used. Can be one of the following:<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ML&quot;</span><span> </span>for maximum likelihood,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;GLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for (normal theory) generalized least squares,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for weighted least squares (sometimes called ADF estimation),<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULS&quot;</span><span> </span>for unweighted least squares,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;DWLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for diagonally weighted least squares, and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;DLS&quot;</span><span> </span>for distributionally-weighted least squares. These are the main options that affect the estimation. For convenience, the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ML&quot;</span><span> </span>option can be extended as<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLM&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMV&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMVS&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLF&quot;</span>, and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span>. The estimation will still be plain<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ML&quot;</span>, but now with robust standard errors and a robust (scaled) test statistic. <u>For<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLM&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMV&quot;</span>,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMVS&quot;</span>, classic robust standard errors are used (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">se=&quot;robust.sem&quot;</span>)</u>; for<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLF&quot;</span>, standard errors are based on first-order derivatives (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">information = &quot;first.order&quot;</span>);<u> for<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span>, ‘Huber-White’ robust standard errors are used (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">se=&quot;robust.huber.white&quot;</span>). </u>In addition,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLM&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a Satorra-Bentler scaled (mean adjusted) test statistic (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;satorra.bentler&quot;</span>) ,<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMVS&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a mean and variance adjusted test statistic (Satterthwaite style) (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;mean.var.adjusted&quot;</span>),<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLMV&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a mean and variance adjusted test statistic (scaled and shifted) (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;scaled.shifted&quot;</span>), and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span><span> </span>will compute a test statistic which is asymptotically equivalent to the Yuan-Bentler T2-star test statistic (<span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">test=&quot;yuan.bentler.mplus&quot;</span>). Analogously, <u>the estimators<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLSM&quot;</span><span> </span>and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLSMV&quot;</span><span> </span>imply the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;DWLS&quot;</span><span> </span>estimator (not the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;WLS&quot;</span><span> </span>estimator) with robust standard errors</u> and a mean or mean and variance adjusted test statistic. Estimators<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULSM&quot;</span><span> </span>and<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULSMV&quot;</span><span> </span>imply the<span> </span><span style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;background-position:0% 0%;background-repeat:repeat;background-image:none;background-size:auto;background-origin:padding-box;background-clip:border-box;color:white\">&quot;ULS&quot;</span><span> </span>estimator with robust standard errors and a mean or mean and variance adjusted test statistic.</p></span></span></div><div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Johan</div></div><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">torsdag den 13. december 2018 kl. 14.11.55 UTC+1 skrev Terrence Jorgensen:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">do you recommend reporting the non-scaled fit statistics/indices over the scaled versions? </div></blockquote><div><br></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>If robust estimation is necessary, then report the scaled statistic and any scaled (or robust, if available) fit indices that are derived from it.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div></div></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div></div></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\"><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792415191701000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0XVZhFUTs32pt3UUaS23P1\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
